# Search

The Jaringan search engine lives at [search.simonharms.xyz](jrg://search.simonharms.xyz).
It indexes public JRG pages whose owners have submitted their domains and
proven ownership via DNS.

## How to get indexed

1. **Publish a jrgidx file** at `/.well-known/jrgidx` on your domain (see
   the spec for the exact format).
2. **Sign and encryption-cap every page** you list in that jrgidx.
3. **Visit the submit page** and enter your domain.
4. **Create a DNS TXT record** at `_jrg-verify.<your-domain>` with the
   token the engine returns.
5. **Click verify** — the engine checks DNS, fetches your jrgidx, and
   indexes your pages.

## Querying

Connect with the Jaringan browser:

```
jaringan-browser open jrg://search.simonharms.xyz/
```

Type a query, hit search. Results show the title, URL, and a relevance
score. All indexed pages are signed, so you can trust the source.

## Self-hosting

The engine is a single Rust binary. Run it on any host, point a public
hostname at it, and you're a search node:

```bash
jaringan-search serve --bind 127.0.0.1:7071 --data-dir /var/lib/jaringan-search
```

See `docs/spec/jrg-search.md` for the full spec and `docs/spec/jrg-proxy.md`
for the vhost router that routes multiple services through a single port.

## In-page search (local M5)

Local `.jrg` files can also expose their own search form:

```
? q label="Search query" value="action" placeholder="Search docs"
! find label="Find" method="GET" target="/search"
```

When the browser opens this from a local file root, hitting `Find` searches
that root and renders a result list. See `docs/examples/search-form.jrg`.

